<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chinese Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chinese Civil War was fought between the Nationalist government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, in two phases from 1927 to 1937 and from 1945 to 1949, interrupted by the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The Communists under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> won and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in October 1949, while the Nationalists withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war.jpg" alt="Chinese Civil War" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Siping03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the 1911 Revolution China fell into a power struggle following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a>'s death in 1916. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>'s Kuomintang (KMT) set up a government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> to rival the warlords. When Western countries ignored his requests for aid, Sun turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and in 1923 the Sun–Joffe Manifesto pledged Soviet assistance. In 1926 and 1927 the KMT and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> (CCP), allied in the First United Front, carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a> against the warlords. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> massacre of 1927 the CCP launched an uprising at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanchang">Nanchang</a> on 1 August 1927, the date from which armed conflict continued intermittently.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first phase lasted from 1927 to 1937. In October 1934 the CCP broke out of the ring of blockhouses that encircled it, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>, which lasted a year. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> held to a policy of 'first internal pacification, then external resistance'. From 1937, when Japan launched its full-scale invasion, hostilities were mostly put on hold under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-united-front">Second United Front</a>, though cooperation was minimal and clashes remained common.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s defeat approached, both sides rushed to take over occupied areas. In 1945 the Communists had 1.2 million troops with 2 million militia, and Soviet forces handed them Northeast China and captured Japanese weapons. On 20 July 1946 Chiang launched a large assault on Communist territory in the north with 113 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a>. The CCP gave up territory to preserve its forces, and after a year the balance had shifted. The Liaoshen, Huaihai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pingjin-campaign">Pingjin campaigns</a> ended in Communist victory in January 1949.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Communists proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in October 1949, and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> government retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> on 7 December 1949. Historians explain the Communist victory by the hyperinflation, corruption and loss of trust under the Nationalist government, and by the CCP's land reform, which won peasant support. Both sides committed atrocities in which millions of non-combatants were killed. After 1927 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> killed between 300,000 and one million people, mainly peasants, in the White Terror, and the Communists executed 2,000 to 3,000 members of the Futian battalion in December 1930. After 1949, official figures of 1954 give at least 2.6 million arrested and 712,000 executed in the Campaign to Suppress Counterrevolutionaries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chinese Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Chinese Civil War</b>, at the time of the peaceful takeover of Hunan in 1949, Song Yang and another musician, Yi Yang, arranged the song for four-part harmony in Changsha; by then it was already widely sung.</blockquote><a href="/songs/red-sun-in-the-sky">Red Sun in the Sky</a></li><li><blockquote>The song was popular in the communist base area of Yan'an but was heard less after the party won the <b>Chinese Civil War</b> and founded the People's Republic of China in 1949, possibly because some senior leaders disagreed with its portrayal of Mao as China's saviour.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red</a></li></ul></section>
